invariant: INV-26
file: quic_aes_hw.c
catches: aes_equiv_test
reason: the AES=hw AES-256 key expansion stops one round key short, so
  its fifteenth round key is whatever the caller's buffer held and the
  fourteenth round of every block runs under it. That is the round count
  of TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384's schedule, and CBMC cannot read the
  instructions this file runs, so bin/aes_equiv_test is what holds it: it
  expands every key under both implementations and compares all fifteen
  round keys byte for byte before it compares a block. The edit sits
  outside the two architecture arms, so an ARMv8 runner and an x86-64
  one each compile and catch it.

--- old
    expand(key, AES_256_KEY, round_keys, (size_t)AES_256_ROUND_KEYS * AES_BLOCK);
--- new
    expand(key, AES_256_KEY, round_keys, (size_t)(AES_256_ROUND_KEYS - 1) * AES_BLOCK);
